swift_retain_x<N> and swift_release_x<N>, as Xcode 27.0's LLVM calls them (B-170, docs/formats/build.md "Objective-C
literals, runtime entrypoints and `this`"): when the module flag "Swift DirectReg Version" is not 0, which Xcode's
swift-frontend writes for the platforms that have the entry points (.github/swift 0003), a call to swift_retain or
swift_release takes its object in the register the allocator gives it, as 0015 does for objc_retain and
objc_release, for every arm64 target; never without the flag, nor on x86_64 (clang_arc_register_experiments.py).
Both kinds are called where SelectionDAG selects the instructions, so for arm64_32 at -O0 too (neither GlobalISel nor
FastISel selects it there; the releases only, as Xcode's LLVM), and not in a function GlobalISel selected (-global-isel
above -O0), with the register kept as allocated (no copy propagation into it). An objc_retain
lowered from llvm.objc.retain is not marked returned, as in Xcode's IR after PreISelIntrinsicLowering, so the code
after it takes its result as ObjCARCContract left the uses (also on macOS and before iOS 16), and 0015's rewriting of
later uses after the call, which stood in for that, goes: after swift_retain the object stays in its register.
